Z||000000||David DeVoe||10-31-2001||11:16 AM||||Drat!! I've got storage compartment lid edge curl, bane of just about every Fiero I've looked at. As an optimist I'm hoping theres a fix for it and that a forum member knows what it is. I'm guessing contact cement and lots of cussing.<BR>Dave||||reg||5
Z||000001||jch82||10-31-2001||11:46 AM||||I used good old fashioned super glue on mine. It is still holding and this was done back when I got the car the first of April.<P>Good Luck!<P>Josh  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jch82_2.jpg"> ||||reg||14
Z||000002||avengador1||10-31-2001||11:50 AM||||I read somewhere that you could use contact cement and some clamps to fix it.<P>------------------<BR>Black with Silver 1987 GT. NEFA Member Custom Class.||||reg||1
Z||000003||falconhulk||10-31-2001||11:52 AM||||what storage compartment?  You mean the trunk?  What is curling?  I dont have this that i know of so I am curious||||reg||1
Z||000004||Ali_Gulf_Fiero||10-31-2001||12:13 PM||||Some glues won't hold unless you soften the vinyl with applied heat. It's due to warpage (I know you know that - I'm just establishing the scene).I first tried epoxy. Too messy and too much uniform clamping required. The best way to attack vinyl is with a heat gun or hair dryer. Apply the heat to the underside of the vinyl. There's no set technique that I can distill, it's just a matter of whether or not you get a feel for how to work with the material, and whether or not too much sideways stretching has occurred. Friggin' cheap mid-80's GM interiors!  I was able to reshape my console door edge. I was unable to reform the radio cluster surround. best of luck to you tho. I found a mint condition one at a salvage yard. Eventually you'll start calling the yard your second home!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>my cat's sitting in my scrambled eggs.||||reg||1
Z||000005||MinnGreenGT||10-31-2001||12:38 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by falconhulk:<BR><B>what storage compartment?  You mean the trunk?  What is curling?  I dont have this that i know of so I am curious</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>They're referring the the "glove box" built into the back of the center console (between the seats).  It, as well as a few other dash parts, warp over time- usually due to temperature changes over extended periods of time.||||reg||1
Z||000006||David DeVoe||10-31-2001||12:54 PM||||Thanks for the info and you're right junkyards can be your friend. I figured I'd try my heat gun and some contact adhesive, maybe wrapping the whole thing with some nylon strapping I've got to hold it in place for awhile. Also gritting my teeth stategically and using words I learned in the Navy. <BR>Dave||||reg||1
Z||000007||skitime||10-31-2001||01:04 PM||||The only one that I found works is the Super Glue.  Its great since you don't need to hold the piece in position very long.  It allows you to word in small areas as you work your way around the piece. No clamping needed and it has held up very well through the summer heat.<P>------------------<BR><UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>Red 88 GT T-TOP<BR><LI>Red 86 GT<BR></UL>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature-2.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Nashco||10-31-2001||01:09 PM||||I also used super glue.  Almost every edge of my interior was curling up.  Some edges were curling very badly, as a result of the foam inside expanding beyond the limits of the vinyl.  I used an exacto knife and cut a fair amount of foam out all around the edges, just enough so the vinyl would conform to where it needed to go again.  I then used superglue, a little bit at a time, for all the edges.  I had a ton to do, used a whole bottle, but this still seemed the quickest, easiest, and cheapest way to do it.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000009||California Kid||10-31-2001||05:13 PM||||Here's a mod to the shifter trim, in case you can't get glue to work in this area (which happened in my case).  Make metal straps, paint and use black torx or allen head bolts.<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Shifter_Trim_Mod.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by California Kid (edited 10-31-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||Ali_Gulf_Fiero||10-31-2001||06:21 PM||||Hey Cal kid - Very nice job you did! What kind of paint did you use on the vinyl? I'd like a uniform black dash and console. Too many subtle and un-subtle shades of grey and sundamage-black for my sensitive tastes! Does the paint crack over soft padded places on the center console cover for example??||||reg||5
Z||000011||tgowens||10-31-2001||07:44 PM||||I use super glue, paint sticks and clamps. I have done many of the shop's customer's cars as well as my own this way.<P>Put a small amount of super glue under the vinyl and place a paint stick on the outside and another on the inside and clamp together for about five minutes. I have never had a car come back because the repair came loose.....<P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.whgraphics.com" TARGET=_blank>West Highland Graphics & Design</A><BR><A HREF="http://www.cfog-i.org" TARGET=_blank>Classic Fiero Owners Group International</A>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Gerald||11-01-2001||12:10 PM||||Um those are all great ideas but I took a different route. It does not appeal to some people but I like it. Or maybe I just tell myself that. But in any case I found some cosmetictly appealing 7mm self taping screws. They are not like any of the 7mm screws used on the fiero. They have a taller head on them.I used them on my glove box, cig lighter plate, my heater/stero pod and soon to be for the rest of the center console. I think they give the interior an industrial look and they dont look too out of place. When I find another center consel assembly I will go the glue way and install that but the screws will do for now.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Gerald||11-01-2001||12:11 PM||||Um those are all great ideas but I took a different route. It does not appeal to some people but I like it. Or maybe I just tell myself that. But in any case I found some cosmetically appealing 7mm self taping screws. They are not like any of the 7mm screws used on the fiero. They have a taller head on them.I used them on my glove box, cig lighter plate, my heater/stereo pod and soon to be for the rest of the center counsel. I think they give the interior an industrial look and they don't look too out of place. When I find another center counsel assembly I will go the glue way and install that but the screws will do for now.||||reg||1
